# cgroup v2 OOM-kill acceptance unit (plan §7.3).
#
# Plan §7.3's last bullet is "与健康检测联动:检测到 OOM kill 后进入重启策略" - an OOM
# kill has to be *detected* and the unit then follows its restart policy, rather
# than being left dead with a bare `SIGKILL` as the only clue.
#
# This unit makes the kernel's cgroup OOM killer fire for real, which is the
# half no host test can do: with `CgroupMemoryMax=16M`, the shell below doubles a
# string until the cgroup cannot satisfy the allocation, and the memory
# controller then kills a process *inside this cgroup* - the system-wide OOM
# killer would say nothing about the unit's limit.
#
# Three things are asserted from the console, and only the first is the kernel's:
#
#   Memory cgroup out of memory: Killed process ...   the kernel's own record
#   killed by the cgroup OOM killer (memory.max=...)  rinit naming the cause
#   oom.service will restart in                       the restart policy
#
# The counter rinit reads (`memory.events`' `oom_kill`) is what makes the
# middle line honest. `SIGKILL` is also how a stop timeout and a shutdown
# arrive, so the signal alone cannot say an OOM kill happened - and because the
# cgroup directory is *kept* across a restart (the restart path does not clean
# up), a bare "the counter is not zero" would be wrong too. rinit therefore
# compares it against the value it read before the service could allocate.
#
# `StartLimitBurst=2` bounds the demo: the first OOM schedules a restart, the
# second trips the breaker, so this cannot churn for the rest of the run.
# `Type=simple` matters - the shell is Active when the kill lands, which is the
# state the restart policy is written against.
[Unit]
Description=cgroup v2 OOM-kill demo (started on demand)

[Service]
Type=simple
CgroupMemoryMax=16M
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=1
StartLimitBurst=2
StartLimitIntervalSec=300
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c "x=a; while :; do x=$x$x; done"
